        "math_academy_id": "q-37777",
        "problem": "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+4x+3,$ then ${A}^{4}=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{4}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+4x+3,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $4x$ | $+$ | $13$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+4x+3\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{4}\\,\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | $+$ | $4{x}^{3}$ | $+$ | $3{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $4{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $3{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $4{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $16{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $12x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $13{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $12x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $13{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $52x$ | $+$ | $39$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  | $-$ | $40x$ | $-$ | $39$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{4}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4} & =q(x)p(x)+(-40x-39),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{2}-4x+13.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=q(A)p(A)+(-40A-39I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & =q(A)p(A)+(-40A-39I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-40A-39I) \\\\  & =-40A-39I.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
